1:1-1 (* EXPECT-ERROR: the pool of n is not known here, and the callee may allocate in it *)
2:1-15 (* This is the object-pool form through a local that says no IN.  Rule 2\n   of the pool elision plan hands a callee the pool of the object behind\n   a VAR pointer argument, named from the argument's root.  A local\n   without an IN clause names the FRAME.  That is right for a frame\n   allocation and wrong for a pool one.  Here `n := NEW (pool, Node)`\n   lives in pool, `SetName (n, s)` allocates the name in Build's frame,\n   `root := n` keeps the node, and Build returns.  The first read of\n   root.name is a heap-use-after-free (ASan), and the plain build prints\n   NULs and whatever was allocated over it.  Ast.SetA's first UTF cut\n   did exactly this for every node's name on 2026-10-23, and --check\n   said nothing.  The checker's rule-2 refusals named a value parameter\n   and an OWN parameter as roots without a pool, not a bare local\n   holding nobody's object.  It does since the same night; `n : PTR Node\n   IN pool` is the sound spelling. *)
comments=2
